<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.2.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02358</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.2.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>1f069264-ff75-4d18-9273-df1552dc6e37</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<para>For questions 1 - 5, the standard form of an equation is \(ax^2+bx+c\). For each expression, find the missing constant \(c\) that would make the expression a perfect square.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="74779890-74be-4c6e-abac-e9c6c8418736"><problem><para>
      <para>\(x^2−6x+ \underline{\;\;c\;\;}\)</para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>9</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is: 9 can be added to make the expression a perfect square. </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><!--3a-->
<para>For each equation in questions 11 - 14, use the following scenario.</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>Jada looks at Mai's work and is confused. She doesn't see how Mai got her answer.</para>
  <para>Complete Mai's missing steps by answering the questions to help Jada see how Mai solved the equation.</para>
</blockquote>
<para>\(x^2+12x=13\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>\(x^2\;+\;12x\; +\) \(\_\_\_\_\_\) \(=\;13\;+\) \(\_\_\_\_\_\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> \((x+6)^2=49\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>\(\sqrt{(x+6)^2}=\sqrt{49}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> \(x+6= \pm\) \(\_\_\_\_\_\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 - </emphasis>\(x+6=\) \(\_\_\_\_\_\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6 -</emphasis> \(x\;+\;6=\) \(-\) \(\_\_\_\_\_\)</para>
<para>\(x=1\) or \(x=-13\)</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="daff277b-a353-4044-96ee-ad9ea59081f8"><problem><para>
      <para>What is the missing term in Step 1?</para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>36</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is 36.
      </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><para>For questions 15 - 20, solve choose the equivalent equation with a perfect square on one side.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="a68974c6-171a-48d6-8ba7-8ee9f75aedfa"><problem><para>
      <para>\(x^2+8x=2\) </para>
      </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\((x−7)^2=54\)</para><para>\((x+5)^2=12\)</para><para>\((x−10)^2=91\)</para><para>\((x+4)^2=18\)</para><para>\((x+1)^2=1\)</para><para>\((x+2)^2=9\)</para></problem><solution><para>\((x+4)^2=18\)</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is: \((x+4)^2=18\). </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><!--5a-->
<para>For questions 21-23, solve each equation by completing the square, then choose the <emphasis>two</emphasis> roots.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="01ba74f0-4495-49ae-a6f3-b852b9226b0d"><problem><para>
      <para>\(x^2−6x+5=12\)</para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>["\\(x = -1\\)", "\\(x = 7\\)"]</para><para>
      <para>The correct answers are: \(x=-1\) and \(x=7\). </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
